#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147715 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a80ccfb3-b089-5b2e-9eec-171909e2d54e" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Variation of Parameters to a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ba126c7-4e22-553c-9b22-1df2d637eacb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{4}e^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144246} {:answer/id #uuid "df6e6972-9ebd-5146-a47c-cfdbd25b5d14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{5}e^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144247} {:answer/id #uuid "1b9af7a3-1432-51c5-9ba3-7b1bffafe05a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{3}e^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144248} {:answer/id #uuid "3ea90a58-ea10-589c-9876-1ead1ea153a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{5}e^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144249} {:answer/id #uuid "60c68c89-9b60-50dc-8bee-73fd19d60e2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{3}e^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144250}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b9af7a3-1432-51c5-9ba3-7b1bffafe05a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{3}e^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144248} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0ce8940-3562-58a2-9700-253003b6ac23" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144245}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cd8f1873-f8da-5860-a81c-597e89738a7c" :question/math-academy-id "q-337211" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}+2y={x}^{3}{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}={e}^{2x}.$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that ${u}_{2}(x)=\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ find the function $g.$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)={x}^{3}{e}^{5x}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})({e}^{2x})^{′}-({e}^{x})^{′}({e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(2{e}^{2x})-({e}^{x})({e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{3x}-{e}^{3x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}({e}^{2x})^{′}={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{2}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{1}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{x}}=-{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ (-{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{x}){e}^{x}+2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ -{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′} & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{e}^{5x}}{{e}^{2x}} \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{2}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{3}{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\int {x}^{3}{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)={x}^{3}{e}^{3x}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147716 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e0d4530-bf50-526c-a975-941ee1fed9a0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a VOP Equation Using the Derivative Constraint"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93e34ce4-9987-55a5-9955-acfa42388e81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u_{1}e^{x} + u_{2}e^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144252} {:answer/id #uuid "dbafcc83-2b56-5017-bb48-91d99d0d6ff6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u_{1}(e^{x})^{′} + u_{2}(e^{2x})^{′}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144253} {:answer/id #uuid "078fa81b-67c6-5b9b-96f5-04597354c5cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u_{1}^{′}e^{x} + u_{2}^{′}e^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144254} {:answer/id #uuid "4a37c2d0-659b-59c1-8422-69fae24f1b51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u_{1}^{′}(e^{x})^{′} + u_{2}^{′}(e^{2x})^{′}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144255}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93e34ce4-9987-55a5-9955-acfa42388e81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u_{1}e^{x} + u_{2}e^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144252} :answer-field/id #uuid "774454c7-1d4d-578c-a1fe-ec2b897f888b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144251} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b14cc531-e196-5acb-be48-cdacc35f645a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u_{1}^{′}e^{x} + u_{2}^{′}e^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144257} {:answer/id #uuid "e6375770-83a8-5248-9407-c226720b6c02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u_{1}e^{x} + u_{2}e^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144258} {:answer/id #uuid "425ccdf5-90ac-5926-9ecf-45d48d0b1b67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u_{1}^{′}(e^{x})^{′} + u_{2}^{′}(e^{2x})^{′}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144259} {:answer/id #uuid "7e740fdf-d6a9-545a-80b5-12d4fc85ddd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u_{1}(e^{x})^{′} + u_{2}(e^{2x})^{′}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144260}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e740fdf-d6a9-545a-80b5-12d4fc85ddd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u_{1}(e^{x})^{′} + u_{2}(e^{2x})^{′}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144260} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a0e6a27-b1c4-53ca-bc55-dfb6b35b80ea" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144256} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73c1cbc7-3279-5ccb-ace7-12d508f30069" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{1}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375196} {:answer/id #uuid "3deb4c1e-9926-54e1-810b-efb2cc582d97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{1}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2{u}_{2}{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375197} {:answer/id #uuid "debf3b8e-15da-576d-87ba-74c513409625" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{1}{e}^{x}+2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375198}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3deb4c1e-9926-54e1-810b-efb2cc582d97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{1}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2{u}_{2}{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375197} :answer-field/id #uuid "edc81094-026b-539a-9900-9417504fd7ff" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375195} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ec90432-0c4d-5cf7-a735-85ea8078ab4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375200} {:answer/id #uuid "eda61926-7b14-595f-b959-30146c35edb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}-{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375201} {:answer/id #uuid "28db3942-e17f-5a74-b90e-8afe0e90311c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375202} {:answer/id #uuid "72de5187-940c-55bf-b961-4664abdb9fee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375203}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ec90432-0c4d-5cf7-a735-85ea8078ab4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375200} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0d49228-0c2d-5a0d-8181-4065b19b2556" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375199}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fff75877-42f9-5ba2-9ee3-ada5f9ead0c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-337218" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}+2y={x}^{3}{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}={e}^{2x}.$ \n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={{field-1}}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}={u}_{1}(x),{u}_{2}={u}_{2}(x),$ that satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)={{field-2}}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{p}$ with respect to $x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following constraint equation relating ${u}_{1}^{′}$ and ${u}_{2}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a second-order linear inhomogeneous differential equation, the variation of parameters method requires that we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x)\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}.\n$$\n\nHere, ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nIn this case, we're told that the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}={e}^{x},\\,{y}_{2}={e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our expression for ${y}_{p},$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our constraint equation for ${y}_{p}^{′},$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}={u}_{1}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{2x})^{′}.\n$$\n\nWe can form a constraint that relates ${u}_{1}^{′}$ and ${u}_{2}^{′}$ by differentiating ${y}_{p}$ and equating this expression to our constraint on ${y}_{p}^{′}.$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{p}$ with respect to $x$ using the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ={({u}_{1}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{2x})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({u}_{1}{e}^{x})}^{′}+{({u}_{2}{e}^{2x})}^{′} \\\\  & ={u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{1}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}({e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & ={u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{1}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2{u}_{2}{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we equate our two expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{1}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2{u}_{2}{e}^{2x} & ={u}_{1}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{1}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2{u}_{2}{e}^{2x} & ={u}_{1}{e}^{x}+2{u}_{2}{e}^{2x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+\\cancel{{u}_{1}{e}^{x}}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}+\\cancel{2{u}_{2}{e}^{2x}} & =\\cancel{{u}_{1}{e}^{x}}+\\cancel{2{u}_{2}{e}^{2x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147718 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a80ccfb3-b089-5b2e-9eec-171909e2d54e" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Variation of Parameters to a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a06de49a-846d-5941-88a0-6806c42c9792" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{3}e^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144272} {:answer/id #uuid "a3fc9699-ed45-5ba4-9a1f-4e99e31ddcad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2}e^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144273} {:answer/id #uuid "a73c9895-f6a1-5230-83e0-130d8b6b9901" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{4}e^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144274} {:answer/id #uuid "ace1886e-7dc3-5ed8-b16c-eddfb9a8c8c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{3}e^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144275} {:answer/id #uuid "68881899-8f98-5a2d-a9ff-a9597afebc6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{4}e^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144276}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ace1886e-7dc3-5ed8-b16c-eddfb9a8c8c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{3}e^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144275} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b9b85d1-0f35-52dc-8c0e-aea0058f63c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186144271}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8a41e469-6fd2-5c20-b908-502549c78a20" :question/math-academy-id "q-337332" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}+2y={x}^{3}{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}={e}^{2x}.$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that ${u}_{1}(x)=-\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ find the function $g.$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)={x}^{3}{e}^{5x}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})({e}^{2x})^{′}-({e}^{x})^{′}({e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(2{e}^{2x})-({e}^{x})({e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{3x}-{e}^{3x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{({e}^{x})}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{({e}^{2x})}^{′}={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{1}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{2}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}}=-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+2(-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}){e}^{2x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}-2{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ -{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′} & =-{x}^{3}{e}^{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{1}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -{x}^{3}{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{1} & =-\\int {x}^{3}{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)={x}^{3}{e}^{4x}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375204 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e0d4530-bf50-526c-a975-941ee1fed9a0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a VOP Equation Using the Derivative Constraint"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28b27848-74db-5f6f-9b3f-37f6717faf44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375206} {:answer/id #uuid "6eccbecd-c1b4-5f97-a790-72b0262799d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x))^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x))^{′}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375207} {:answer/id #uuid "329d6a5e-7c7d-5820-a71a-bb870c55bc58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)+{u}_{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375208} {:answer/id #uuid "771e5ec2-eaa2-5547-818e-b09edca5baf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x))^{′}+{u}_{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x))^{′}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "329d6a5e-7c7d-5820-a71a-bb870c55bc58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)+{u}_{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375208} :answer-field/id #uuid "c19b8a73-7da6-5660-baed-0d87bca6110d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375205} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10dc9d60-a28d-5358-9bf5-b7e179f9138e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375211} {:answer/id #uuid "26413810-ab43-51a9-b21c-7dd28aea8c9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x))^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x))^{′}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375212} {:answer/id #uuid "3fd9f1d5-ec39-53c2-bb8b-1d2e4b8571a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)+{u}_{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375213} {:answer/id #uuid "682c1b89-e015-51ee-a6ee-c87fc69dad32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x))^{′}+{u}_{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x))^{′}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375214}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "682c1b89-e015-51ee-a6ee-c87fc69dad32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x))^{′}+{u}_{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x))^{′}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375214} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb1afd26-3019-5952-84da-e99686886706" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375210} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63afda9a-3c7a-5e21-b149-77c2075adc71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)+5{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)-5{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375216} {:answer/id #uuid "b0b60f11-2385-5241-9184-a92c9b9e2e3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)+{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)-5{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375217} {:answer/id #uuid "f3f7c532-5947-5704-9