:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375219}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fd532329-8030-524a-ae6c-589a2f040dc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-337944" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+25y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x).$ \n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={{field-1}}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}={u}_{1}(x),{u}_{2}={u}_{2}(x),$ that satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)={{field-2}}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{p}$ with respect to $x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following constraint equation relating ${u}_{1}^{′}$ and ${u}_{2}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a second-order linear inhomogeneous differential equation, the variation of parameters method requires that we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x)\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}.\n$$\n\nHere, ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nIn this case, we're told that the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x),\\,{y}_{2}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our expression for ${y}_{p},$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)+{u}_{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our constraint equation for ${y}_{p}^{′},$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}={u}_{1}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x))^{′}+{u}_{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x))^{′}.\n$$\n\nWe can form a constraint that relates ${u}_{1}^{′}$ and ${u}_{2}^{′}$ by differentiating ${y}_{p}$ and equating this expression to our constraint on ${y}_{p}^{′}.$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{p}$ with respect to $x$ using the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ={({u}_{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)+{u}_{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x))}^{′} \\\\  & ={({u}_{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x))}^{′}+{({u}_{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x))}^{′} \\\\  & ={u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)+{u}_{1}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x))^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)+{u}_{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x))^{′} \\\\  & ={u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)+5{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)-5{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we equate our two expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)+5{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)-5{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x) & ={u}_{1}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x))^{′}+{u}_{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x))^{′} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)+5{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)-5{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x) & =5{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)-5{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)+\\cancel{5{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)}+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)-\\cancel{5{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)} & =\\cancel{5{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)}-\\cancel{5{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375224 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a80ccfb3-b089-5b2e-9eec-171909e2d54e" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Variation of Parameters to a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1950ab90-1d6f-5147-a9e7-4b43c9855536" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375226} {:answer/id #uuid "9f1b6b65-625d-5be2-a00c-8c977ea3d9db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375227} {:answer/id #uuid "aeb3507f-7592-55ca-9885-d5867f7b554e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375228} {:answer/id #uuid "7d008150-bd2e-5e5c-9038-e0e1533e442c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375229} {:answer/id #uuid "550285e3-3ab8-5dad-a69a-923a4961d51a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f1b6b65-625d-5be2-a00c-8c977ea3d9db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375227} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a92ca98-a8bd-56e6-b20e-f3ac00c4282d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375225}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3c0d58c7-9f59-569f-a97b-eacd91a0dfff" :question/math-academy-id "q-338355" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+2y={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-x}$ and ${y}_{2}={e}^{-2x}.$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that ${u}_{2}(x)=-\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ find the function $g.$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)={x}^{2}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{-x})({e}^{-2x})^{′}-({e}^{-x})^{′}({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{-x})(-2{e}^{-2x})-(-{e}^{-x})({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-3x}+{e}^{-3x} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{({e}^{-x})}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{({e}^{-2x})}^{′}={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}-\\,{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{2}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{1}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}}{{e}^{-x}}=-{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\,{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ -\\,(-{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-x}){e}^{-x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ -\\,{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′} & =-{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{2}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{2} & =-\\int {x}^{2}{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)={x}^{2}{e}^{2x}.$"}]}
